{
 "id": "l09",
 "group": "lecture",
 "num": 9,
 "title": "L9 · Spine & Thoracic Cage",
 "subtitle": "Vertebrae, regional features, spinal curves, ribs, sternum",
 "lectures": "Lecture 9",
 "voice": "af_heart",
 "scenes": [
  {
   "lec": "L9",
   "title": "Five regions of the spine",
   "img": "L9_01.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Cervical 7",
    "Thoracic 12",
    "Lumbar 5",
    "Sacrum",
    "Coccyx"
   ],
   "start": 0.4,
   "tstart": 0.4,
   "tend": 1.93,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "The spine has 26 vertebrae. The top 24 move.",
     "start": 2.63,
     "end": 6.2
    },
    {
     "text": "**Cervical**: 7, in the neck.",
     "start": 6.65,
     "end": 8.38
    },
    {
     "text": "**Thoracic**: 12, in the upper back.",
     "start": 8.83,
     "end": 10.89
    },
    {
     "text": "**Lumbar**: 5, in the low back.",
     "start": 11.34,
     "end": 13.22
    },
    {
     "text": "The **sacrum** is 5 fused vertebrae. The **coccyx** is 4 fused.",
     "start": 13.67,
     "end": 17.72
    },
    {
     "text": "Breakfast at 7, lunch at 12, dinner at 5.",
     "start": 18.17,
     "end": 21.11
    }
   ],
   "end": 22.21
  },
  {
   "lec": "L9 · Vertebra",
   "title": "A typical vertebra",
   "img": "L9_02.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Body",
    "Pedicle",
    "Lamina",
    "Spinous process"
   ],
   "start": 22.21,
   "tstart": 22.21,
   "tend": 23.36,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "The **body** is the big front part. It's largest in the lumbar spine.",
     "start": 24.06,
     "end": 28.11
    },
    {
     "text": "Two **pedicles** project back from the body.",
     "start": 28.56,
     "end": 30.84
    },
    {
     "text": "Two **laminae** meet in the midline.",
     "start": 31.29,
     "end": 33.02
    },
    {
     "text": "Together they form the **spinous process**, the bump you feel down the back.",
     "start": 33.47,
     "end": 37.23
    }
   ],
   "end": 38.33
  },
  {
   "lec": "L9 · Vertebra",
   "title": "Vertebral processes and foramen",
   "img": "L9_04.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Articular processes",
    "Transverse process",
    "Vertebral foramen"
   ],
   "start": 38.33,
   "tstart": 38.33,
   "tend": 40.38,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Superior **articular processes** meet the vertebra above.",
     "start": 41.08,
     "end": 44.71
    },
    {
     "text": "Inferior articular processes meet the vertebra below.",
     "start": 45.16,
     "end": 48.72
    },
    {
     "text": "**Transverse processes** project to the sides.",
     "start": 49.17,
     "end": 51.64
    },
    {
     "text": "The **vertebral foramen** is the hole for the spinal cord.",
     "start": 52.09,
     "end": 55.24
    }
   ],
   "end": 56.34
  },
  {
   "lec": "L9 · Discs",
   "title": "Intervertebral discs",
   "img": "L9_06.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Fibrocartilage",
    "Weight bearing"
   ],
   "start": 56.34,
   "tstart": 56.34,
   "tend": 57.6,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Discs sit between the vertebrae.",
     "start": 58.3,
     "end": 60.07
    },
    {
     "text": "They are **fibrocartilage**, the cartilage that resists compression.",
     "start": 60.52,
     "end": 64.13
    },
    {
     "text": "They bear weight.",
     "start": 64.58,
     "end": 65.49
    }
   ],
   "end": 66.59
  },
  {
   "lec": "L9 · Cervical",
   "title": "Cervical vertebrae",
   "img": "L9_07.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Bifid",
    "Transverse foramina"
   ],
   "start": 66.59,
   "tstart": 66.59,
   "tend": 67.71,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Cervical spinous processes are **bifid**, split in two.",
     "start": 68.41,
     "end": 71.65
    },
    {
     "text": "They have two **transverse foramina**. The vertebral arteries run through them.",
     "start": 72.1,
     "end": 76.22
    },
    {
     "text": "Their bodies are small. Their foramen is triangular.",
     "start": 76.67,
     "end": 79.82
    }
   ],
   "end": 80.92
  },
  {
   "lec": "L9 · Cervical",
   "title": "Atlas and axis",
   "img": "L9_12.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Atlas C1",
    "Axis C2",
    "Dens"
   ],
   "start": 80.92,
   "tstart": 80.92,
   "tend": 82.08,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "The **atlas** is C1. It's a ring with no body and no spinous process.",
     "start": 82.78,
     "end": 87.27
    },
    {
     "text": "It meets the occipital condyles.",
     "start": 87.72,
     "end": 89.61
    },
    {
     "text": "The **axis** is C2.",
     "start": 90.06,
     "end": 91.42
    },
    {
     "text": "Its **dens**, or odontoid process, points up into the atlas.",
     "start": 91.87,
     "end": 95.57
    }
   ],
   "end": 96.67
  },
  {
   "lec": "L9 · Thoracic and lumbar",
   "title": "Thoracic and lumbar vertebrae",
   "img": "L9_08.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Rib facets",
    "Round foramen",
    "Spatulated"
   ],
   "start": 96.67,
   "tstart": 96.67,
   "tend": 98.55,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "Only thoracic vertebrae have **rib facets**.",
     "start": 99.25,
     "end": 101.72
    },
    {
     "text": "Their foramen is round. Their spinous processes point down.",
     "start": 102.17,
     "end": 105.63
    },
    {
     "text": "Lumbar vertebrae have large bodies and a triangular foramen.",
     "start": 106.08,
     "end": 110.08
    },
    {
     "text": "Their spinous process is wide and **spatulated**.",
     "start": 110.53,
     "end": 113.31
    }
   ],
   "end": 114.41
  },
  {
   "lec": "L9 · Sacrum",
   "title": "Sacrum and coccyx",
   "img": "L9_13.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Median sacral crest",
    "Sacral foramina",
    "Coccyx"
   ],
   "start": 114.41,
   "tstart": 114.41,
   "tend": 115.65,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "The sacrum is one triangle made of five fused vertebrae.",
     "start": 116.35,
     "end": 119.87
    },
    {
     "text": "The **median sacral crest** is what's left of the spinous processes.",
     "start": 120.32,
     "end": 124.12
    },
    {
     "text": "It has four pairs of **sacral foramina**.",
     "start": 124.57,
     "end": 126.71
    },
    {
     "text": "The **coccyx** is the tailbone at its tip.",
     "start": 127.16,
     "end": 129.27
    }
   ],
   "end": 130.37
  },
  {
   "lec": "L9 · Curves",
   "title": "Spinal curves",
   "img": "L9_15.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Scoliosis",
    "Lordosis",
    "Kyphosis"
   ],
   "start": 130.37,
   "tstart": 130.37,
   "tend": 131.36,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "From behind, the spine should be straight. A sideways curve is **scoliosis**.",
     "start": 132.06,
     "end": 136.6
    },
    {
     "text": "From the side, it curves.",
     "start": 137.05,
     "end": 138.36
    },
    {
     "text": "**Lordosis** is in the cervical and lumbar spine. It's a secondary curve, formed after birth.",
     "start": 138.81,
     "end": 144.49
    },
    {
     "text": "**Kyphosis** is in the thoracic and sacral spine. It's a primary curve, there from birth.",
     "start": 144.94,
     "end": 150.33
    }
   ],
   "end": 151.43
  },
  {
   "lec": "L9 · Ribs",
   "title": "True, false and floating ribs",
   "img": "L9_17.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "True ribs",
    "False ribs",
    "Floating ribs"
   ],
   "start": 151.43,
   "tstart": 151.43,
   "tend": 153.26,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "There are 12 pairs of ribs.",
     "start": 153.96,
     "end": 155.53
    },
    {
     "text": "**True ribs** are 1 to 7. Their cartilage joins the sternum directly.",
     "start": 155.98,
     "end": 160.4
    },
    {
     "text": "**False ribs** are 8 to 12. Their cartilage joins rib 7's, or nothing.",
     "start": 160.85,
     "end": 165.48
    },
    {
     "text": "**Floating ribs** are 11 and 12. They have no cartilage.",
     "start": 165.93,
     "end": 169.5
    }
   ],
   "end": 170.6
  },
  {
   "lec": "L9 · Ribs",
   "title": "Parts of a rib",
   "img": "L9_18.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Head",
    "Neck",
    "Tubercle",
    "Angle"
   ],
   "start": 170.6,
   "tstart": 170.6,
   "tend": 171.56,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "The **head** meets the vertebral body.",
     "start": 172.26,
     "end": 174.23
    },
    {
     "text": "The **neck** is between the head and the tubercle.",
     "start": 174.68,
     "end": 176.9
    },
    {
     "text": "The **tubercle** meets the transverse process.",
     "start": 177.35,
     "end": 179.78
    },
    {
     "text": "The body is the main part. The **angle** is where it curves forward.",
     "start": 180.23,
     "end": 183.85
    }
   ],
   "end": 184.95
  },
  {
   "lec": "L9 · Sternum",
   "title": "The sternum",
   "img": "L9_20.jpg",
   "terms": [
    "Manubrium",
    "Sternal angle",
    "Body",
    "Xiphoid"
   ],
   "start": 184.95,
   "tstart": 184.95,
   "tend": 185.71,
   "lines": [
    {
     "text": "The **manubrium** is on top. Rib 1 joins it. The jugular notch is at its top.",
     "start": 186.41,
     "end": 191.52
    },
    {
     "text": "The **sternal angle**, or angle of Louis, is where rib 2 attaches.",
     "start": 191.97,
     "end": 195.6
    },
    {
     "text": "The **body** is the largest part. Ribs 3 to 7 join it.",
     "start": 196.05,
     "end": 199.76
    },
    {
     "text": "The **xiphoid** process is at the bottom. No ribs attach.",
     "start": 200.21,
     "end": 203.59
    }
   ],
   "end": 204.69
  }
 ],
 "duration": 204.69,
 "audio": "stories/l09.mp3"
}